Moment of inertial of solid cylinder, about the axis passing through its centre of mass and perpendicular to its plane
<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fts_eamcet%252F4PghgO8a-clr0OMOttGc2KUQU70UyPQ9OLD5T6ZrT_M.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fts_eamcet%2F4PghgO8a-clr0OMOttGc2KUQU70UyPQ9OLD5T6ZrT_M.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fts_eamcet%2F4PghgO8a-clr0OMOttGc2KUQU70UyPQ9OLD5T6ZrT_M.original.fullsize.png?alt=media"><br>

I_{X Y}=M\left(\frac{l^2}{12}+\frac{R^2}{4}\right)

From theorem of parallel axis, moment of inertial of cylinder ahout an axis passing through its edge and perpendicular to it plane.

\begin{aligned}
I_{A B} & =I_{X Y}+M\left(\frac{l}{2}\right)^2 \\
\therefore \quad I_{A B} & =M\left(\frac{l^2}{12}+\frac{R^2}{4}\right)+M \frac{l^2}{4} \\
& =M\left(\frac{l^2}{3}+\frac{R^2}{4}\right)
\end{aligned}

But $\quad l=6 R$
\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad I_{A B} & =M\left[\frac{(6 R)^2}{3}+\frac{R^2}{4}\right] \\
& =M\left[12 R^2+\frac{R^2}{4}\right] \\
& =M\left(\frac{49 R^2}{4}\right)=\frac{49 M R^2}{4}
\end{aligned}
